fix(sigstore): persist rekor's search index, and rebuild the one already lost (#219)

MEASURED, then fixed. Every commit signed before the last container recreate
failed verification at check 2, and the record was never gone:

  log index 300 artifact : c6a01d2d...4832
  verify computes        : c6a01d2d...4832

Same digest, and that index's integrated time is the commit's certificate
validity start. Trillian held every entry. Nothing could find one.

The index is Redis, and sigstore.yml ran it with --appendonly no --save "" and
no volume, so it lived in the container's writable layer. Rekor indexes an
entry when the entry is WRITTEN and never afterwards, so an emptied index is
never repopulated -- it silently holds only what has been added since the last
recreate. That is the sharp date boundary this started from.

The comment above the service said "losing it loses search, never a proof".
That premise is false and is now corrected in place: a verifier proves
inclusion by ASKING the log, so an index that cannot answer is a proof that
cannot be made. I5 says a third party verifies with no access to this system's
database, and check 2 is the half the transparency log answers.

Two parts, because preventing it does not repair it. The service gets an
append-only file on a named volume. scripts/rekor-reindex.sh walks the log and
rebuilds the keys, building under a temporary namespace and renaming over the
live keys so an interrupted run leaves the old index serving.

Observed failing first, live: after a recreate the index held 2 keys and a
commit that verified an hour earlier reported FAILED. After the reindex, 1056
keys from 528 entries and a commit signed three days ago verifies. After a
second deliberate recreate, 1056 keys survive and it still verifies.

Two defects in the script itself are written into it rather than quietly
fixed. `docker exec -i` reads the CALLER's stdin, so calling it inside a while
read loop consumed the loop's input and the first run renamed exactly one key.
And the entries endpoint honours one logIndex; repeating it returns a single
entry, which under-collected 528 entries into 106 keys without erroring. The
second is the one worth the comment -- it did not fail, it indexed a fifth of
the log and reported success.

Drove VER-013, VER-014 and VER-015, proposed in doc 07 first.

